ex(1,2.5,'Pair solutions with opposite x; among x=0 pair (y,z) with (−y,−z). Only the origin remains unpaired.')
q(2,4,'Geometry',r'A square of side $s$ is folded along a line through its centre so that its visible outline is a nonagon. Let $p$ be the sum of the eight boundary sides other than the fold. Express the nonagon’s area using $p$ and $s$.','The short solution recognizes that all eight non-fold boundary sides have the same perpendicular distance from the centre.',['Area & dissection','Transformations'],r'''Join the centre $O$ to the vertices of the folded outline. Every boundary side other than the fold lies on either an original square side or its reflection in the fold. Its distance from $O$ is therefore $s/2$. The triangles with those eight bases partition the folded region (the fold contributes zero area). Consequently
$$A=\frac12\cdot\frac s2\cdot p=\boxed{\frac{ps}{4}}.$$''')
q(3,4,'Number Theory',r'Find all primes $p$ and nonnegative integers $m,n$ satisfying $m^3=1+p^n$.','The two factors of m³−1 share only a factor of three; a valuation check eliminates the apparent p=3 family.',['Diophantine equations','Primes','Valuations'],r'''If $n=0$ then $m^3=2$, impossible. Thus $m>1$ and
$$p^n=(m-1)(m^2+m+1).$$
If $m-1=1$, then $m=2$ and $(p,n)=(7,1)$. Otherwise both factors are divisible by $p$, which must divide $3$ after setting $m\equiv1\pmod p$. Hence $p=3$ and $m=1+3k$.

But $m^2+m+1=3(1+3k+3k^2)$ has exactly one factor of $3$, so, being a power of $3$, it must equal $3$. This is impossible for $m>1$. The only triple is $(p,m,n)=(7,2,1)$.''')
q(4,4,'Geometry',r'A point starts at the origin and travels along straight segments in any of six directions spaced $60^\circ$ apart. Describe all endpoints reachable with total travel at most $10$, and find the perimeter of that region.','The hexagonal boundary is intuitive, but a complete proof needs both an attainable path and a lower bound ruling out every exterior point.',['Combinatorial geometry','Coordinates & vectors','Quadrilaterals & polygons'],r'''Let $u_1,\ldots,u_6$ be the six allowed unit direction vectors. An endpoint has the form $\sum t_i u_i$, where $t_i\ge0$ and $\sum t_i\le10$. It therefore lies in the convex hull of $10u_1,\ldots,10u_6$ (the origin also belongs to this hull).

Conversely any point in that convex hull is a convex combination $\sum\lambda_i10u_i$, with nonnegative coefficients summing to $1$. Following the corresponding six segments reaches it with total length $10$. Thus the region is a closed regular hexagon of circumradius, and side length, $10$. Its perimeter is $\boxed{60}$.''')
q(5,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'For $n\ge2$, count integer tuples $(a_1,\ldots,a_n)$ such that $a_1=0$, every later entry differs by at most $1$ from some earlier entry, and exactly $n-1$ distinct integers occur.','The visited integers always form an interval. Isolating the unique repetition then makes the extensions independently countable.',['Counting','Sequences & recurrences'],r'''There is exactly one step $m\in\{2,\ldots,n\}$ that repeats an earlier value. Before and after that step, the distinct values form an integer interval; each new value must extend one of its two ends by one.

For fixed $m$, the repeated entry has $m-1$ choices. Each of the other $n-2$ steps independently extends the upper or lower end, giving $2^{n-2}$ choices. Hence the total is
$$\sum_{m=2}^n(m-1)2^{n-2}=\boxed{2^{n-3}n(n-1)}.$$''')
q(6,5,'Algebra',r'Real numbers $a,b,c$ satisfy $a^2b^2+18abc>4b^3+4a^3c+27c^2$. Prove $a^2>3b$.','Treating the expression as a quadratic in c reveals a discriminant that factors as a cube; choosing that variable is the key step.',['Inequalities','Algebraic identities'],r'''The hypothesis says
$$27c^2+(4a^3-18ab)c+4b^3-a^2b^2<0.$$
A quadratic with positive leading coefficient can be negative only if its discriminant is strictly positive. Here that discriminant is
$$(4a^3-18ab)^2-108(4b^3-a^2b^2)=16(a^2-3b)^3.$$
Thus $(a^2-3b)^3>0$, which gives the result.''')
paper('Sweden',2012,sw+'finalproblemen.pdf',sw+'loesningsfoerslag.pdf','2012-11-24')
