q(5,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'The vertices of a regular $13$-gon are coloured with three colours. Prove that some three vertices of one colour form an isosceles triangle.','Pigeonhole gives five vertices of one colour; the substantial part is proving that a five-point subset cannot avoid every three-term progression modulo13.',['Pigeonhole principle','Colouring','Combinatorial geometry'])
q(6,4.5,'Geometry',r'A circle is inscribed in a trapezoid. Prove that the intersection of the diagonals lies on the diameter perpendicular to the parallel sides.','Equal tangent lengths translate the common height into a product identity, which is exactly the ratio needed for the two diagonals to meet on the diameter.',['Circles & tangency','Quadrilaterals & polygons','Similarity & congruence'])

