ex(1,2,'Nonnegative coefficients sum to seven, so evaluation at ten is ordinary decimal notation; its digits determine the polynomial immediately.')
q(2,4.5,'Geometry',r'Three circles are pairwise externally tangent, and their centres lie on a circle of radius $R$. Prove that the sum of their areas is less than $4\pi R^2$.','A useful split at radius R turns the geometric configuration into a short but non-obvious bound on three squared radii.',['Circles & tangency','Inequalities'],r'''Write the radii as $\rho\ge r\ge s>0$. Every sum of two radii is a chord length of the circle of centres, hence is at most $2R$. If $\rho\le R$, the sum of squared radii is at most $3R^2<4R^2$. Otherwise $r<R$, since $\rho+r\le2R$. Therefore
$$\rho^2+r^2+s^2\le(2R-r)^2+2r^2=4R^2-r(4R-3r)<4R^2.$$
Multiplying by $\pi$ proves the claim.''')
q(3,4,'Algebra',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ satisfying $f(f(x+y)-f(x-y))=xy$ for all real $x,y$.','Surjectivity obtained from one specialization conflicts with the nonnegative range forced by another; the nested arguments obscure that short contradiction.',['Functional equations'],r'''There are no such functions. Setting $x=y=0$ gives $f(0)=0$. Setting $y=1$ shows that $f$ is surjective, because its displayed value is $x$. But setting $y=x$ gives $f(f(2x))=x^2\ge0$. Since $f(2x)$ ranges over all real numbers, $f$ is everywhere nonnegative, contradicting surjectivity.''')
q(4,4.5,'Geometry',r'A square is divided into finitely many triangles. Prove that the sum of the diameters of the triangles’ incircles exceeds the side length of the square.','Combining the area–inradius formula with a uniform perimeter bound makes an arbitrary triangulation manageable.',['Area & dissection','Circles & tangency','Inequalities'],r'''Let the square have side $s$. Each triangle inside it has perimeter less than $4s$: the sum of the absolute horizontal edge displacements is twice its horizontal range, at most $2s$, and likewise vertically. Euclidean edge length is at most the sum of those two displacements, with a strict inequality for at least one edge of a nondegenerate triangle.

For each triangle, area equals inradius times semiperimeter, and so is less than $2s$ times its inradius. Summing gives $s^2<s\sum d_i$, where $d_i$ are the incircle diameters. Thus $\sum d_i>s$.''')
q(5,4,'Combinatorics',r'Twenty students each attempt six problems. Must there be five students and two problems such that all five solved both problems, or all five solved neither?','The key is a balanced counterexample built from all three-element subsets, followed by a precise incidence count.',['Counting','Algorithms & constructions'],r'''No. Assign to the twenty students the twenty distinct three-element subsets of the six problems, and let each student solve exactly that subset. For any pair of problems, exactly $\binom41=4$ students solve both, and exactly $\binom43=4$ solve neither. Thus neither requested group of five exists.''')
q(6,6,'Number Theory',r'Determine all odd primes $p,q$ and positive integers $x,y$ satisfying $x^p+y^q=pq$.','An exponential size estimate first forces a unit variable; Fermat and a lift modulo q² then collapse the two primes to one.',['Diophantine equations','Primes','Modular arithmetic','Inequalities'],r'''For every integer $k\ge3$, $2^k>k^2/2$. Thus, if $x,y\ge2$,
$$x^p+y^q\ge2^p+2^q>(p^2+q^2)/2\ge pq,$$
a contradiction. Suppose $x=1$, the other case being symmetric. Then $1+y^q=pq$. Modulo $q$, Fermat gives $y\equiv-1\pmod q$. Writing $y=-1+kq$ and expanding modulo $q^2$ gives $1+y^q\equiv0\pmod{q^2}$, so $p=q$.

Now $q^2=1+y^q$. Necessarily $y\ge2$. For integers $q\ge5$, $2^q>q^2$ (induction from $q=5$), impossible. Hence $q=3$ and $y=2$. The complete answer is $(p,q,x,y)=(3,3,1,2),(3,3,2,1)$.''')
paper('Sweden',2011,sw+'Final_20111.pdf',sw+'Finalloesn2011.pdf','2011-11-19')
ex(1,3.5,'Ordering the three factorials bounds the smallest against 3n!, leaving only the two adjacent factorial cases; straightforward substitution finishes.')
ex(2,2.5,'Two elementary triangle angle comparisons from the longest distances show the requested angle is acute.')
ex(3,3.5,'Choose the largest variable and compare two cyclic equations; the opposite monotonicities force equality throughout.')
q(4,4,'Number Theory',r'Three cities $A,B,C$ are joined by some nonnegative numbers of direct links. A route uses either one link or two links through the third city. There are $43$ routes from $A$ to $B$ and $29$ from $B$ to $C$. What are the possible numbers of routes from $A$ to $C$?','Translating the route counts gives a coupled Diophantine system; adding and subtracting produces restrictive divisor conditions.',['Diophantine equations','Divisibility & GCD'],r'''Let $x,y,z$ count the direct links $AB,BC,AC$. Then $x+yz=43$ and $y+xz=29$, so
$$(x+y)(z+1)=72,\qquad(y-x)(z-1)=14.$$
If $z=0$, then $(x,y)=(43,29)$ and $z+xy=1247$. The value $z=1$ is impossible. For $z\ge2$, the positive divisor $z-1$ of $14$ leaves $z=2,3,8,15$; the first equation eliminates $15$, and $3$ gives nonintegral $x,y$. The remaining solutions are $(x,y,z)=(5,19,2),(3,5,8)$, giving $97$ and $23$ routes. All three configurations are valid, so the answer is $\boxed{23,97,1247}$.''')
q(5,4.5,'Combinatorics',r'A token starts in the centre cell of an $11\times11$ board. On each turn the first player moves it one cell horizontally or vertically; then the second player places a wall along one cell edge. Walls cannot be crossed. The first player wins by leaving the board. Can the second player prevent escape?','Four preparatory corner walls make every later boundary threat uniquely blockable; spotting the preparation is the strategic step.',['Games & strategies','Invariants'],r'''Yes. During the first four turns, place one wall on an outside edge at each of the four corner cells. The token needs at least six moves to leave the board from the centre, so these moves are safe.

Every boundary cell now has at most one unblocked edge leading outside. After each later move, if the token is in a boundary cell, block that edge if it is still open. Otherwise place any available wall. The token therefore never starts a move next to an unblocked outside edge. It cannot escape; eventually all outside edges can be blocked.''')
q(6,4.5,'Algebra',r'''How many functions $f:\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\to\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ satisfy $f(0)=2011$, $f(1)=111$, and
$$f(\max\{x+y+2,xy\})=\min\{f(x+y),f(xy+2)\}$$
for all nonnegative integers $x,y$?''','Two small substitutions force the entire infinite tail to be constant; checking the exceptional initial arguments is essential to count all functions.',['Functional equations','Sequences & recurrences'],r'''Putting $y=0$ gives $f(n+2)=\min\{f(n),f(2)\}$, so $f(2),f(3),f(4),\ldots$ alternates between $c=f(2)$ and $\min(111,c)$. Putting $x=y=1$ gives $f(4)=\min(f(2),f(3))$, forcing $c\le111$. Thus the whole tail from $2$ onwards is $c$.

Conversely, every integer $c\in[0,111]$ works. The left-hand argument is always at least $2$, hence its value is $c$. The argument $xy+2$ also gives $c$, while $f(x+y)$ is either $2011$, $111$ or $c$, all at least $c$. Therefore the answer is $\boxed{112}$.''')
paper('Sweden',2010,sw+'Final10.pdf',sw+'Fisvar10.pdf','2010-11-20')
ex(1,2.5,'Areas make the side lengths proportional to the reciprocal altitudes; 1,1/2,1/3 violate the triangle inequality.')
q(2,4.5,'Geometry',r'Take four distinct lines of the form $y=kx-k^2$, where their parameters $k$ are distinct integers. Choose four distinct intersection points so that each of the four lines contains exactly two chosen points. Write their coordinates as $(x_i,y_i)$ with $x_1\le x_2\le x_3\le x_4$. Prove $x_1+x_4=x_2+x_3$ and $y_1y_4=y_2y_3$.','The incidence condition is a four-cycle; identifying the smallest and largest intersection sums as opposite edges then proves both identities at once.',['Coordinates & vectors','Graphs'],r'''Lines with parameters $a,b$ intersect at $(a+b,ab)$; no three of these lines are concurrent. Regard each chosen point as an edge joining its two line parameters. Every vertex has degree two, so the four chosen edges form a four-cycle.

Call the edge with smallest sum $ab$, its opposite edge $cd$, and its neighbours $ac,bd$. From $a+b\le a+c$ and $a+b\le b+d$ we get $b\le c$ and $a\le d$. Consequently $c+d\ge a+c,b+d$, so $cd$ has largest sum. Opposite edge pairs use every parameter exactly once. Their coordinate sums are both $a+b+c+d$, and their products of second coordinates are both $abcd$. These are the required identities.''')
ex(3,3.5,'The factor theorem gives (n−1)(n−2) as a divisor of the prime value; only n=3 survives and the quadratic itself supplies the example.')
q(4,4.5,'Number Theory',r'Let $a_1=2010$. For $n\ge2$, let $a_n$ be the least integer greater than $a_{n-1}$ that is divisible by $n$. Prove that $a_{100},a_{101},a_{102},\ldots$ form an arithmetic progression.','A crude cumulative bound is enough to enter a stable range; the main insight is to express a_n as n times a fixed multiplier.',['Sequences & recurrences','Divisibility & GCD','Induction'],r'''Each increment is at most $n$, so
$$a_{100}\le2010+\sum_{n=2}^{100}n=7059<10000.$$
Write $a_{100}=100k$, where $1\le k<100$. If $a_n=nk$ for $n\ge100$, then
$$(n+1)(k-1)<nk<(n+1)k.$$
Thus the least multiple of $n+1$ greater than $a_n$ is $(n+1)k$. Induction gives $a_n=nk$ for every $n\ge100$, an arithmetic progression with common difference $k$.''')
q(5,4,'Geometry',r'The side lengths $a,b,c$ of a triangle satisfy $(a+b+c)(a+b-c)=2b^2$. Find the greatest possible angle opposite $a$, and determine the triangle’s angles when that maximum is attained.','Recasting the condition through the cosine and sine rules leads to a clean bound on tan A.',['Triangle geometry','Trigonometry','Extremal principle'],r'''Write $A,B,C$ for the opposite angles. The condition rearranges to $a^2=b^2+c^2-2ab$. The cosine rule therefore gives $\cos A=a/c>0$. By the sine rule, $a/c=\sin A/\sin C$, so $\tan A=\sin C\le1$. Hence $A\le45^\circ$. Equality occurs exactly when $C=90^\circ$, giving $A=B=45^\circ$; this triangle satisfies the original condition.''')
q(6,5,'Combinatorics',r'Finitely many cells of an infinite square grid are red. Prove that they can all be covered by finitely many grid-aligned squares with pairwise disjoint interiors, so that the proportion of red cells in each square lies between $1/5$ and $4/5$, inclusive.','A repeated four-way subdivision controls the overshoot in density; proving termination is the crucial complement to the construction.',['Algorithms & constructions','Extremal principle','Combinatorial geometry'],r'''If there are no red cells, use no squares. Otherwise enclose them in a sufficiently large grid square of power-of-two side length whose red density is below $1/5$. Split this square into four equal squares. Discard empty children, keep children of density at least $1/5$, and subdivide the remaining children again.

A child of a subdivided square has density less than $4/5$, because its parent has density below $1/5$. Therefore every kept square has density in the required interval. The process stops: a one-cell nonempty square would have density $1$, and so cannot arise as a child of a square still requiring subdivision. Squares kept at different stages have disjoint interiors, and every red cell is eventually in a kept square.''')
paper('Sweden',2009,sw+'Final09-2.pdf',sw+'Fisvar09-1.pdf','2009-11-21')
q(1,4,'Geometry',r'Can five square mats with side lengths $2,2,2.1,2.5,2.5$ fit inside a square room of side $6$ without overlapping? Rotation is allowed.','A rotated middle square fits between two unequal pairs; giving exact coordinates verifies a configuration that a rough sketch can easily misjudge.',['Combinatorial geometry','Coordinates & vectors','Algorithms & constructions'],r'''Yes. Use the room $[0,6]^2$. Put the side-$2.5$ squares in $[0,2.5]\times[0,2.5]$ and $[0,2.5]\times[3.5,6]$. Put the side-$2$ squares in $[4,6]\times[0,2]$ and $[4,6]\times[4,6]$.

The diamond with vertices $(2,3),(3.5,1.5),(5,3),(3.5,4.5)$ has no interior overlap with these four squares: each neighbouring corner lies on its boundary, which is $|x-3.5|+|y-3|=1.5$. Its side is $3/\sqrt2>2.1$, so it contains the remaining mat.''')
ex(2,2.5,'Nonpositive x is immediately impossible; three AM-GM inequalities multiply to the desired bound for positive x, with equality only at one.')
ex(3,3.5,'The probability equation makes n²−n+1 a square, but for n>1 it lies strictly between (n−1)² and n².')
q(4,4,'Number Theory',r'Find all integer solutions of $x+x^3=5y^2$.','Coprimality splits the factors into two square patterns; one is excluded between consecutive squares and the other by fourth-power residues.',['Diophantine equations','Divisibility & GCD','Modular arithmetic'],r'''Since $x(1+x^2)=5y^2$, we have $x\ge0$. If $x=0$, then $y=0$. For $x>0$, the factors $x$ and $x^2+1$ are coprime. Hence either $x=5s^2$ and $x^2+1=t^2$, or $x=s^2$ and $x^2+1=5t^2$, for positive integers $s,t$.

The first is impossible because $x^2<x^2+1<(x+1)^2$. The second gives $s^4+1\equiv0\pmod5$, whereas a fourth power modulo $5$ is $0$ or $1$. Thus the only solution is $\boxed{(0,0)}$.''')
q(5,4,'Geometry',r'A semicircle of radius $1$ has diameter $AB$ and centre $O$. A point $P$ lies inside it on the radius perpendicular to $AB$, with $OP=d$. The ray $AP$ meets the semicircle again at $C$, and the line through $P$ perpendicular to $PC$ meets the semicircle at $D$. Through $C$ draw a parallel to $PD$, and through $D$ a parallel to $PC$; they meet at $E$. Find $OE$ in terms of $d$.','Recognizing the rectangle gives a short squared-distance identity, avoiding a lengthy coordinate computation.',['Coordinates & vectors','Circles & tangency','Quadrilaterals & polygons'],r'''The quadrilateral $PCED$ is a rectangle. For any point $O$, the sums of squared distances to opposite vertices of a rectangle are equal:
$$OP^2+OE^2=OC^2+OD^2.$$
This follows directly by expanding the four squared vector lengths. Here $OC=OD=1$ and $OP=d$, so $\boxed{OE=\sqrt{2-d^2}}$.''')
q(6,5,'Combinatorics',r'Each cell of a $17\times17$ board contains a coin, initially heads up. A move flips five consecutive coins in a horizontal, vertical or either diagonal direction. Can every coin be made tails up?','A five-colouring that intersects every permitted segment once turns the goal into incompatible parity requirements.',['Colouring','Invariants','Parity'],r'''No. Number rows and columns from $0$ to $16$, and colour cell $(i,j)$ by $i+2j\pmod5$. Along any allowed direction the colour changes by a nonzero residue modulo $5$, so every move flips exactly one coin of each colour.

Four colour classes contain $58$ cells and the other contains $57$ (counting the residue classes of the row and column indices gives these sizes). If every coin ended tails up, the total number of flips in each colour class would have the parity of its size. But it is the same number—the number of moves—for every colour. The differing parities make this impossible.''')
paper('Sweden',2008,sw+'Final08.pdf',sw+'Fisvar08.pdf','2008-11-22')
ex(1,3.5,'Two similar-triangle ratios for the inscribed rhombus add to one, giving the harmonic-product side length directly.')
