q(4,4,'Geometry',r'A convex polygon has interior angles that are positive integer multiples of $36^\circ$. (a) What is the largest possible number of sides? (b) Prove that if it has more than five sides, some two sides are parallel.','Exterior-angle quantization bounds the number of sides and restricts all unoriented side directions to five classes.',['Quadrilaterals & polygons','Pigeonhole principle'],r'''Every exterior angle is a positive multiple of $36^\circ$ and their sum is $360^\circ$. Thus there are at most ten sides; a regular decagon attains ten.

As we traverse the boundary, every side direction differs from the first by an integer multiple of $36^\circ$. Modulo $180^\circ$ there are only five such directions. With more than five sides, the pigeonhole principle gives two in the same class, hence parallel.''')
q(5,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'A number $2008$ is written down. Repeatedly, player A splits the current number $n>1$ into two positive integers; player B erases one of them and play continues with the other. Play stops at $1$. B wins if two of the erased numbers are equal; otherwise A wins. Who can force a win?','Classifying starting values by powers of two reveals the strategy; the proof must handle unequal power-of-two children and an eventual deviation from repeated halving.',['Games & strategies','Induction','Binary representation'])
q(6,7,'Combinatorics',r'Among all sets of distinct positive integers whose sum is $100$, find the greatest possible product.','Several local exchanges constrain the minimum element and missing integers. Completing that structural classification is much harder than guessing the nearly consecutive maximizing set.',['Extremal principle','Inequalities','Algorithms & constructions'])
