q(2,4,'Combinatorics',r'There are $n$ people holding respectively $1,2,\ldots,n$ flowers. The person initially holding one flower meets every other person once, in an order of their choice. At each meeting they receive one flower if the other person currently holds more. What is the smallest possible final number of flowers they can hold?','A lower bound comes from counting everyone initially above the eventual total; reverse order attains it.',['Extremal principle','Algorithms & constructions','Counting'],r'''Let the final total be $N$. Each of the $n-N$ people who initially held more than $N$ must have given a flower, so $N-1\ge n-N$. Thus $N\ge\lceil(n+1)/2\rceil=\lfloor n/2\rfloor+1$.

Meet the other people in descending order $n,n-1,\ldots,2$. At the $k$th meeting, if a flower has been received at every earlier meeting, the traveller holds $k$ and the other person holds $n-k+1$. A flower is received exactly while $2k<n+1$, giving $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ gifts. All later holdings are too small to give one. This attains the bound.''')
ex(3,2.5,'The sine rule expresses the circumradius factor and the cosine rule expands each cotangent; summing cancels the cross terms.')
q(4,5.5,'Combinatorics',r'A finite collection of closed arcs of a circle has the property that every two arcs meet. Prove that two diametrically opposite points can be chosen so that every arc contains at least one of them.','After discarding semicircle-sized arcs, a shortest-overlap endpoint argument coordinates the remaining arcs across opposite half-circles.',['Combinatorial geometry','Extremal principle'])
q(5,5,'Combinatorics',r'For each $n=2,3,\ldots,2007$, two players alternately place a domino on two empty adjacent cells of a $1\times n$ strip. A player unable to move loses. Prove that the first player has a winning strategy for at least $1505$ of these values of $n$.','Even boards have a symmetry strategy; pairing neighbouring odd board lengths exploits how an end move transfers a losing position.',['Games & strategies','Symmetry','Counting'],r'''For every even $n$, place a domino in the centre, leaving two equal strips, and mirror every subsequent move. This gives $1003$ winning values. The board $n=3$ is also immediately winning.

If an odd length $m$ is losing for the first player, then length $m+2$ is winning: an end domino leaves length $m$ to the opponent. Therefore at least one length in each pair $(5,7),(9,11),\ldots,(2005,2007)$ is winning. There are $501$ pairs, so at least $1003+1+501=1505$ values are winning.''')
