q('1(b)',4,'Combinatorics',r'Ten boxes each hold at most $2021$ index cards. For each chicken, copies of its card may be placed in several boxes. Any seven boxes together must contain a card for every chicken. What is the largest possible number of chickens?','Four-copy redundancy gives a capacity bound, and a balanced cyclic construction must attain the integer bound despite the leftover spaces.',['Counting','Algorithms & constructions','Extremal principle'],r'''Each chicken needs cards in at least four different boxes, or losing three boxes could lose all its cards. Thus the number of chickens is at most $\lfloor20210/4\rfloor=5052$.

For a block of ten chickens, put their cards in the ten cyclic blocks of four consecutive boxes. This uses four slots in every box. Repeat for $505$ blocks, covering $5050$ chickens and using $2020$ slots in each box. Use eight of the ten remaining slots for four copies each of two more chickens. Every chicken is represented in four boxes, so any seven boxes contain it. The maximum is $\boxed{5052}$.''')
