q('1',4,'Number Theory',r'For odd positive $n$, let $n!!=n(n-2)\cdots3\cdot1$. How many different residues modulo $1000$ occur among $1!!,3!!,5!!,\ldots$?','Separating the modulus into eight and125 turns the infinite tail into a short cycle, while the initial values need a careful finite count.',['Modular arithmetic','Valuations','Sequences & recurrences'],r'''For $n\ge25$, $125\mid n!!$. Modulo $8$, successive odd factors repeat $1,3,5,7$, whose product is $1$. Thus the double factorial residues cycle as $1,3,7,1$ modulo $8$. By the Chinese remainder theorem, the tail has exactly three residues modulo $1000$: $625,875,375$.

For $n=1,3,\ldots,23$, successive multiplication modulo $1000$ gives
$$1,3,15,105,945,395,135,25,425,75,575,225.$$
These twelve residues are distinct and none is divisible by $125$. Hence there are $\boxed{15}$ residues altogether.''')
