q('3(a)',4,'Algebra',r'Find all real polynomials $P$ satisfying $P(x)+3P(x+2)=3P(x+1)+P(x+3)$ for every real $x$.','Recognizing a third finite difference turns the translation identity into a degree constraint.',['Polynomials','Finite differences'],r'''Define $\Delta P(x)=P(x+1)-P(x)$. The equation is exactly $\Delta^3P=0$. A nonconstant polynomial of degree $d$ has finite difference of degree $d-1$, since its leading term $ax^d$ contributes $ad x^{d-1}$. Therefore the third difference vanishes precisely for polynomials of degree at most two, including the zero polynomial. The answer is $\boxed{P(x)=ax^2+bx+c}$ with arbitrary real $a,b,c$.''')
q('3(b)',5.5,'Algebra',r'Find all real polynomials $P$ satisfying $\sum_{j=0}^{2018}(-1)^j\binom{2018}{j}P(x+j)=0$ for every real $x$.','The binomially weighted expression must be identified as a high finite difference, with an induction or shift-operator proof and a leading-degree argument.',['Polynomials','Finite differences','Induction'],r'''For $\Delta f(x)=f(x+1)-f(x)$, induction using Pascal’s identity gives
$$\Delta^r f(x)=\sum_{j=0}^r(-1)^{r-j}\binom rj f(x+j).$$
Since $2018$ is even, the equation says $\Delta^{2018}P=0$. Each finite difference lowers the degree of a nonconstant polynomial by exactly one and sends a constant to zero. Thus the solutions are exactly all real polynomials of degree at most $2017$, including zero.''')
q('4(a)',5.5,'Combinatorics',r'An integer sequence is valid if every even entry is followed by half that entry, and every odd entry is followed by an integer differing from it by $1$. Find the shortest possible length of a valid sequence starting at $2018$ and ending at $1$.','A short path is easy to find, but proving minimality requires controlling both choices at every odd value; a decreasing recurrence makes the optimization rigorous.',['Algorithms & constructions','Binary representation','Sequences & recurrences'])
