ex('1(b)',3.5,'After f(0)=1, the substitution (1,−1) forces f(1) or f(−1) to vanish; either gives the full function immediately.')
q('2',4,'Number Theory',r'Let $a_0=2$, $a_1=15$, and $a_{n+2}=15a_{n+1}+16a_n$ for $n\ge0$. Prove that $269$ divides infinitely many terms of the sequence.','Solving the recurrence exposes a power of two whose exponent aligns with Fermat’s theorem and the alternating sign.',['Sequences & recurrences','Modular arithmetic','Primes'],r'''Induction on the recurrence gives $a_n=16^n+(-1)^n$. The number $269$ is prime: none of $2,3,5,7,11,13$ divides it. Fermat therefore gives $2^{268}\equiv1\pmod{269}$.

For any nonnegative integer $t$, take $n=67(2t+1)$. Then $n$ is odd and $4n=268(2t+1)$, so $a_n=2^{4n}-1\equiv0\pmod{269}$. These are infinitely many distinct indices.''')
