q('3(b)',5,'Combinatorics',r'Two players alternately mark previously unmarked triangular cells in an infinite regular triangular grid, using different symbols. Four consecutive cells in a straight grid strip, in any of the three strip directions, form a winning row. Can either player force a win, or can both prevent the opponent from winning?','A hexagonal grouping gives a pairing defence, and a strategy-stealing argument supplies the first player’s defence as well.',['Games & strategies','Pairing','Combinatorial geometry'],notes='The original paper’s diagram specifies the allowed four-cell rows; consult it for the triangular-grid convention.')
q('4',5.5,'Geometry',r'Fix $a>0$ and $0<\alpha<\pi$. Triangle $ABC$ has $BC=a$, $\angle BAC=\alpha$, circumcentre $O$ and orthocentre $H$. A point $P$ lies on the ray from $A$ through $O$. Reflect $P$ in $AC$ and $AB$ to obtain $S,T$. If $S,A,T,H$ are concyclic, prove that $AP$ depends only on $a$ and $\alpha$.','The isogonal relationship between the circumcentre and orthocentre makes AH a diameter of an auxiliary circle; the sine rule then removes the remaining triangle shape.',['Circles & tangency','Centres & cevians','Trigonometry','Transformations'])
