q('1',5.5,'Combinatorics',r'A walking sequence satisfies $a_{i+1}=a_i\pm1$. Prove that there is an integer sequence $b_1,\ldots,b_{2016}$ such that every walking sequence $a_1,\ldots,a_{2016}$ with $1\le a_i\le1010$ has $a_j=b_j$ for some $j$.','A single sweep cannot catch both parity classes. Two opposite sweeps with a repeated peak handle them separately by a discrete crossing argument.',['Parity','Algorithms & constructions','Invariants'],r'''Choose $b_i=i+1$ for $1\le i\le1008$, and $b_i=2018-i$ for $1009\le i\le2016$. Thus the sequence rises from $2$ to $1009$, repeats $1009$, and descends to $2$.

For a walking sequence, the parity of $i+a_i$ is constant. If it is odd, compare the first sweep. At $i=1$, $a_1$ is even, so $a_1\ge b_1$; at $i=1008$, $a_i$ is odd, so $a_i\le1009=b_i$. The even differences $a_i-b_i$ change only by $0$ or $-2$, so one is zero.

If the parity is even, compare the second sweep. At $i=1009$, $a_i$ is odd and at most $1009=b_i$; at $i=2016$, $a_i$ is even and at least $2=b_i$. Here the even differences change only by $0$ or $2$, so again one is zero.''')
ex('2(a)',3,'Adding the equations gives (a−1)(b−1)+(c−1)(d−1)=2, leaving only0+2,1+1,2+0 and three solutions.')
q('2(b)',5.5,'Number Theory',r'Find all nonnegative integers $x,y,z$ satisfying $x^3+2y^3+4z^3=9!$.','Repeated divisibility by two cycles the weighted cubic form; cubic residues modulo nine then reduce the large equation to a tiny bounded search.',['Diophantine equations','Valuations','Modular arithmetic'],r'''Modulo $9$, cubes are $0,\pm1$. The only way a sum $u+2v+4w$ with $u,v,w\in\{0,\pm1\}$ is zero modulo $9$ is $u=v=w=0$. Therefore $3$ divides $x,y,z$. Writing $x=3X,y=3Y,z=3Z$ gives $X^3+2Y^3+4Z^3=13440=2^7\cdot105$.

An even right side forces the variable with coefficient one to be even. Substitute twice that variable and divide by two; the coefficients cycle from $(1,2,4)$ to $(4,1,2)$. Repeating seven times gives $X=8c,Y=4a,Z=4b$ and
$$a^3+2b^3+4c^3=105.$$
Here $a$ is odd, so $a=1$ or $3$, while $b\le3,c\le2$. If $a=1$, the sum is at most $1+54+32=87$. If $c\le1$, it is at most $27+54+4=85$. Thus $a=3,c=2$, leaving $2b^3=46$, impossible. There are no solutions, including cases with a zero variable.''')
q('3(a)',5,'Geometry',r'Three circles with centres $A,B,C$ are pairwise externally tangent. Their contact points on $AB,AC,BC$ are $C^\prime,B^\prime,A^\prime$, respectively. The common tangents at $B^\prime$ and $A^\prime$ meet at $X$. Choose $Y$ so that $BY\perp BX$ and $AY\perp AX$. Prove that $X,Y,C^\prime$ are collinear if and only if $AC=BC$.','Reinterpreting the contact lengths identifies an incenter and an excenter of the triangle of centres; comparing their tangency positions gives the equivalence.',['Circles & tangency','Centres & cevians','Triangle geometry'])
q('3(b)',4.5,'Geometry',r'In acute triangle $ABC$ with $AB<AC$, the internal and external bisectors at $A$ meet line $BC$ at $A_1,A_2$. Reflect $A_2$ in $C$ to obtain $A_3$. A point $Q$ on line $AA_1$ satisfies $\angle A_1QA_3=90^\circ$. Prove $QC\parallel AB$.','A point reflection supplies a diameter through A whose circle passes through Q, converting the claim to an isosceles triangle and angle-bisector symmetry.',['Transformations','Circles & tangency','Triangle geometry'])
q('4',6,'Algebra',r'''Find all functions $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ such that, for all distinct real $x,y$,
$$f(x)f(y)=|x-y|\,f\!\left(\frac{xy+1}{x-y}\right).$$''','A carefully chosen fixed output collapses the equation once nonvanishing is established; the fractional argument and absolute value complicate routine substitutions.',['Functional equations','Algebraic identities'],r'''The zero function works. Suppose $f(z)\ne0$ for some $z$. For every $y\ne z$, solve $xy+zy-xz+1=0$ for $x$. This relation is equivalent to $(xy+1)/(x-y)=z$; it cannot have $x=y$ over the reals. The equation then forces $f(y)\ne0$, so $f$ never vanishes.

For any $y$, choose $x=y+\sqrt{y^2+1}$. Then $x\ne y$ and $(xy+1)/(x-y)=x$. Cancelling $f(x)$ gives $f(y)=|x-y|=\sqrt{y^2+1}$. Finally,
$$(x-y)^2+(xy+1)^2=(x^2+1)(y^2+1)$$
verifies this function. Thus the solutions are $\boxed{f\equiv0}$ and $\boxed{f(x)=\sqrt{x^2+1}}$.''')
