q('2',4,'Geometry',r'Points $P,Q$ lie on sides $BC,CD$ of parallelogram $ABCD$, with $BP=DQ$. Prove that the intersection of lines $BQ$ and $DP$ lies on the bisector of $\angle BAD$.','Extending one line creates two similar-triangle ratios; their equality invokes the converse angle-bisector theorem.',['Quadrilaterals & polygons','Similarity & congruence','Triangle geometry'])
q('3(a)',4,'Combinatorics',r'A grasshopper starts at $(0,0)$ and may jump by $(1,0),(2,0),(1,1),(0,2)$ or $(0,1)$. How many jump sequences reach the line $x+y=2014$?','Collapsing the two-dimensional walk to its coordinate sum gives a second-order recurrence; both one-step and two-step increments must be counted correctly.',['Counting','Sequences & recurrences'],r'''Let $a_n$ count paths ending on $x+y=n$, with $a_0=1$ and $a_{-1}=0$. Two allowed jumps increase the coordinate sum by one, and three increase it by two. Therefore $a_n=2a_{n-1}+3a_{n-2}$.

The formula $a_n=(3^{n+1}+(-1)^n)/4$ satisfies this recurrence and the initial values. Hence the answer is $\boxed{(3^{2015}+1)/4}$.''')
q('3(b)',6,'Combinatorics',r'Nine points lie on a circle. Prove that the $36$ chords joining pairs of them can be coloured with four colours so that every set of four points has all four colours among its six joining chords.','The successful construction labels vertices by a two-dimensional vector space over three residues; four parallel classes and pigeonhole give the required property.',['Colouring','Pigeonhole principle','Modular arithmetic','Algorithms & constructions'],r'''Label the nine points by the pairs $(x,y)\in\{0,1,2\}^2$. Colour a chord by which of the following expressions agrees at its endpoints, modulo $3$:
$$x,\qquad y,\qquad x+y,\qquad x-y.$$
Exactly one agrees. Indeed, two agreements would force both coordinates equal; if neither coordinate agrees, the two nonzero coordinate differences are either equal or opposite, so one of the last two expressions agrees.

For any four chosen vertices, each of these four expressions takes only three possible values. Pigeonhole supplies a pair agreeing in that expression, whose chord has the corresponding colour. Thus every colour occurs.''')
q('4',5,'Number Theory',r'Find all positive integer triples $(a,b,c)$ such that $(32a+3b+48c)/(4abc)$ is an integer.','Divisibility first forces a hidden factor16 in b; the reduced inequality then shows two variables must be one and leaves a controlled finite check.',['Divisibility & GCD','Diophantine equations','Inequalities'],r'''The divisibility first implies $4\mid b$, and then $16\mid b$. Write $b=16t$; the condition becomes $4atc\mid2a+3t+3c$, so
$$4\le\frac2{tc}+\frac3{ac}+\frac3{at}.$$
The right side decreases as any variable increases. At $(a,t,c)=(1,2,2),(2,1,2),(2,2,1)$ it is respectively $3.5,3.25,3.25$, all below four. Therefore at least two variables are one.

If $t=c=1$, then $4a\mid2a+6$, yielding $a=1$ or $3$. If $a=c=1$, then $4t\mid3t+5$, yielding $t=1$ or $5$; the case $a=t=1$ similarly gives $c=1$ or $5$. Substitution confirms
$$\boxed{(a,b,c)=(1,16,1),(3,16,1),(1,80,1),(1,16,5)}.$$''')
norway(2013,'2013-03-07')
ex('1(a)',3,'Completing the square leaves a single quadratic condition on the parameter, giving the interval[−2,6].')
q('1(b)',4,'Algebra',r'Let $a_1=1$ and $a_{n+1}=1+(a_1+\cdots+a_n)/n$. Prove that for every $\beta>0$ there is a positive integer $k$ with $a_k<\beta k$.','Subtracting consecutive defining equations reveals harmonic growth; a quantitative sublinear bound completes the argument without assuming logarithms.',['Sequences & recurrences','Inequalities'],r'''Subtracting the equations for consecutive partial sums gives $a_{n+1}=a_n+1/n$. Thus $a_n=1+\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}1/j$.

For $n\ge2$, Cauchy–Schwarz and $1/j^2\le1/(j(j-1))$ for $j\ge2$ give
$$a_n^2\le n\left(1+\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}\frac1{j^2}\right)<3n.$$
Choose an integer $k\ge2$ with $k>3/\beta^2$. Then $a_k<\sqrt{3k}<\beta k$, as desired.''')
q('2',4.5,'Geometry',r'An acute triangle has longest side $s$. Prove that every point inside the triangle is at distance at most $s/\sqrt3$ from at least one vertex.','The circumcentre partitions the triangle into regions covered by a vertex-centred disk; the largest-angle bound controls the circumradius.',['Triangle geometry','Circles & tangency','Trigonometry'],r'''Let $O$ be the circumcentre and $R$ the circumradius. Since the triangle is acute, $O$ is inside it. Join $O$ to the vertices and side midpoints, forming six triangles. In each small triangle, one original vertex $V$ has distance at most $R$ to all three corners: the corners are $V$, $O$, and a midpoint at distance at most $R$. The closed disk of radius $R$ centred at $V$ is convex, so it covers that entire small triangle. Therefore every point is within $R$ of some original vertex.

The angle opposite the longest side lies between $60^\circ$ and $90^\circ$. The extended sine rule gives $R=s/(2\sin A)\le s/\sqrt3$, completing the proof.''')
q('3',4,'Number Theory',r'For a prime $p\ge5$, write $\frac13+\frac24+\cdots+\frac{p-3}{p-1}=a/b$ with positive integers $a,b$. Prove that $p\mid a$.','Pairing reciprocal residues modulo p proves a harmonic-sum congruence, but the argument must also justify an arbitrary, possibly unreduced fraction.',['Modular arithmetic','Primes','Divisibility & GCD'],r'''Let $D=(p-1)!$. The sum equals
$$\sum_{k=2}^{p-1}\frac{k-2}{k}=p-2\sum_{k=1}^{p-1}\frac1k.$$
Modulo $p$, pair $k$ with $p-k$; the integers $D/k+D/(p-k)$ are divisible by $p$. Hence the sum is $N/D$ with $p\mid N$ and $p\nmid D$.

From $a/b=N/D$, we obtain $aD=bN$. Thus $p\mid aD$, and since $p\nmid D$, necessarily $p\mid a$.''')
ex('4(a)',2.5,'Choose four vertices; each set admits four choices for the first endpoint and two for the third, giving eight times binomial(2013,4).')
q('4(b)',4.5,'Combinatorics',r'Unit cubes form an $a\times b\times c$ rectangular block, where $a,b,c\ge2$. A bee can move between cubes sharing a face. For which triples can it visit every cube exactly once and return to its starting cube?','Checkerboard parity is necessary; sufficiency needs a genuine three-dimensional Hamiltonian construction, reducible to a planar serpentine cycle.',['Graphs','Parity','Algorithms & constructions'])

