q('1(b)',4,'Number Theory',r'Every integer is coloured white or black. If $m$ is white then $m+20$ is white; if $k$ is black then $k+35$ is black. For which integers $n$ can exactly $n$ of $1,2,\ldots,50$ be white?','The one-directional propagation rules for different colours must be combined to force a common period, rather than assuming periodicity at the outset.',['Modular arithmetic','Invariants'],r'''If $m$ is white and $m+5$ black, then $m+40$ is both white (two steps of20) and black (one step of35), impossible. If $m$ is black and $m+5$ white, then $m+105$ is both black (three steps of35) and white (five steps of20). Thus $m$ and $m+5$ always have the same colour.

Each residue class modulo $5$ can be coloured independently, and these colourings satisfy the rules. Each such class occurs ten times in $1,\ldots,50$, so the possible counts are $\boxed{0,10,20,30,40,50}$.''')
q('2(a)',5,'Geometry',r'Two disjoint circles have centres $O_1,O_2$. Draw the two tangent segments from $O_2$ to the first circle, and the two tangent segments from $O_1$ to the second. Prove that there is exactly one circle tangent to all four segments.','Angle bisectors give the candidate circle, but the proof of uniqueness must exclude centres associated with external bisectors and tangency only to extended lines.',['Circles & tangency','Triangle geometry','Constructions & loci'])
q('2(b)',4.5,'Geometry',r'Four circles are pairwise disjoint. For each pair $i,j$, draw the two tangent segments from the centre of either circle to the other. Let $R_{ij}$ be the radius of the circle tangent to these four segments. Prove $R_{12}^{-1}+R_{23}^{-1}+R_{34}^{-1}+R_{14}^{-1}=2(R_{13}^{-1}+R_{24}^{-1})$.','Similar right triangles produce a reciprocal-radius identity independent of the centres’ separation, after which the four-circle formula follows by counting terms.',['Circles & tangency','Similarity & congruence'],r'''Write $r_i$ for the original radius, $d=O_iO_j$, and $X$ for the auxiliary centre on segment $O_iO_j$. By similar right triangles formed with tangent radii,
$$\frac{R_{ij}}{r_j}=\frac{O_iX}{d},\qquad\frac{R_{ij}}{r_i}=\frac{O_jX}{d}.$$
Adding yields $R_{ij}(1/r_i+1/r_j)=1$, or $1/R_{ij}=1/r_i+1/r_j$. In the claimed identity, each reciprocal $1/r_i$ appears twice on either side, so the two sides agree.''')
ex('3(a)',3.5,'The odd product is zero modulo125 and three modulo8; grouping four consecutive odd factors makes the latter calculation immediate, giving875.')
q('3(b)',5.5,'Number Theory',r'Find all positive integers $m$ such that $2^m\mid k^m-1$ for every odd integer $k\ge3$.','The universal quantifier can be attacked with carefully chosen odd bases; an exponential-versus-linear estimate excludes every larger even exponent.',['Valuations','Modular arithmetic','Exponents'],r'''The values $m=1,2,4$ work: odd squares are $1$ modulo $8$, and odd fourth powers are $1$ modulo $16$.

For odd $m\ge3$, choose $k=2^m-1$. Then $k^m\equiv-1\not\equiv1\pmod{2^m}$. For even $m\ge6$, choose $k=2^{m/2}+1$. The binomial theorem gives
$$k^m-1\equiv m2^{m/2}\pmod{2^m}.$$
Divisibility would require $2^{m/2}\mid m$, impossible since $0<m<2^{m/2}$ for even $m\ge6$. Thus the answer is $\boxed{1,2,4}$.''')
ex('4(a)',2,'Expanding the two cubes and applying t^j+t^(−j)≥2 for j=1,2,3 gives16 with equality x=y.')
q('4(b)',4.5,'Algebra',r'Positive numbers $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ have sum at most $10$. Let $a_1=b_1$ and $a_m=sa_{m-1}+b_m$ for $m>1$, where $0\le s<1$. Prove $a_1^2+\cdots+a_n^2\le100/(1-s^2)$.','Expanding the recurrence and applying weighted Cauchy–Schwarz reduces the sum of squares to a geometric series.',['Sequences & recurrences','Inequalities'],r'''We have $a_m=\sum_{j=1}^m s^{m-j}b_j$. Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz gives
$$a_m^2\le\left(\sum_{j=1}^m b_j\right)\left(\sum_{j=1}^m s^{2(m-j)}b_j\right)\le10\sum_{j=1}^m s^{2(m-j)}b_j.$$
Summing and reversing the finite summation order,
$$\sum_{m=1}^n a_m^2\le10\sum_{j=1}^n b_j\sum_{r=0}^{n-j}s^{2r}\le\frac{10\sum b_j}{1-s^2}\le\frac{100}{1-s^2}.$$''')
norway(2011,'2011-03-10')
ex('1(a)',2,'Concatenation preserves the sum of the component integers modulo three, and4022·4023/2 is divisible by three.')
q('1(b)',4.5,'Number Theory',r'Let $N$ be the integer obtained by concatenating $1,2,\ldots,4022$ in order. Put $a_1=N^{2011}$ and, for $i>1$, let $a_i$ be the sum of the decimal digits of $a_{i-1}$. Find $a_4$.','The residue modulo nine is easy, but three successive size reductions are needed to prove the fourth value has already reached its single-digit root.',['Digits & bases','Modular arithmetic','Inequalities'],r'''Modulo $3$, $N\equiv1+\cdots+4022=4022\cdot4023/2\equiv0$. Thus $9\mid a_1$, and every subsequent digit sum is also divisible by $9$.

The number $N$ has fewer than $17000$ digits, so $a_1$ has fewer than $17000\cdot2100=35700000$ digits. Therefore $a_2<9\cdot35700000<10^9$, giving $a_3\le81$. A positive integer at most81 has digit sum less than18, so $0<a_4<18$. Its divisibility by nine forces $\boxed{a_4=9}$.''')
ex('2(a)',3.5,'The given sides have equal sums of opposite squares; applying the cosine rule to the four triangles at the diagonal intersection forces a right angle.')
q('2(b)',4.5,'Geometry',r'The three diagonals $AD,BE,CF$ of a convex hexagon $ABCDEF$ are concurrent. Prove $[ABE][CDA][EFC]=[BCE][DEA][FAC]$, where brackets denote triangle area.','Products of the six small triangle areas cancel by opposite angles, and shared-altitude ratios then lift the identity to the larger triangles.',['Area & dissection','Concurrency & collinearity','Trigonometry'])
q('3(a)',5.5,'Algebra',r'A sequence of positive real numbers satisfies $a_1=1$ and $(m+n)a_{m+n}\le a_m+a_n$ for all positive integers $m,n$. Prove $1/a_{200}>4\cdot10^7$.','Balanced splitting produces rapid decay, but the odd intermediate index25 requires carefully selected neighbouring bounds to reach the stated constant.',['Sequences & recurrences','Inequalities'],r'''Successively using $(m,n)=(1,1),(1,2),(2,2),(3,3),(3,4),(6,6),(6,7)$ gives
$$a_2\le1,\quad a_3\le\frac23,\quad a_4\le\frac12,\quad a_6\le\frac29,\quad a_7\le\frac16,\quad a_{12}\le\frac1{27},\quad a_{13}\le\frac7{234}.$$
Hence $a_{25}\le(a_{12}+a_{13})/25\le47/17550$. Repeatedly taking equal indices gives
$$a_{200}\le\frac{a_{100}}{100}\le\frac{a_{50}}{100\cdot50}\le\frac{a_{25}}{100\cdot50\cdot25}\le\frac{47}{2193750000}.$$
Since $2193750000/47>40000000$, the required strict inequality follows.''')
q('3(b)',4,'Algebra',r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ satisfying $f(xy)\le\tfrac12(f(x)+f(y))$ for every real $x,y$.','Two complementary substitutions force equality away from zero, while the exceptional point retains one-sided freedom.',['Functional equations','Inequalities'],r'''Taking $y=1$ gives $f(x)\le f(1)$ for all $x$. For $x\ne0$, take $y=1/x$ to get
$$f(1)\le\tfrac12(f(x)+f(1/x))\le\tfrac12(f(x)+f(1)),$$
so $f(x)\ge f(1)$. Therefore $f$ equals a constant $c$ on every nonzero input and has $f(0)=d\le c$. Conversely, every such pair of real constants $d\le c$ satisfies the inequality, whether neither, one, or both inputs are zero.''')
q('4(a)',4.5,'Combinatorics',r'A city has $n$ north–south avenues and $n$ east–west streets, numbered $1$ to $n$ in order. Passing through intersection $(k,l)$ costs $kl$, including the initial and final intersections. Find the least cost of a route from $(1,1)$ to $(n,n)$.','Every route crosses each antidiagonal; minimizing its intersection cost gives a lower bound that a boundary route attains.',['Extremal principle','Algorithms & constructions','Inequalities'],r'''Every route must visit a point with coordinate sum $d$ for each integer $d=2,\ldots,2n$. Among allowed points $(k,d-k)$, the product $k(d-k)$ is smallest at an endpoint of the allowed interval for $k$. Those minima occur along the boundary route $(1,1)$ to $(n,1)$ to $(n,n)$, so it is optimal.

Its total cost is
$$\sum_{k=1}^n k+n\sum_{l=2}^n l=\boxed{\frac{n(n^2+2n-1)}2}.$$''')
q('4(b)',5,'Combinatorics',r'In a group of $199$ people, everyone is friends with exactly $100$ others, and friendship is mutual. For which integers $k>1$ must there be $k$ people who are all pairwise friends?','A common-neighbour count guarantees a triangle; a carefully balanced regular graph shows that four mutual friends need not exist.',['Graphs','Pigeonhole principle','Algorithms & constructions'],r'''An edge certainly exists. Its endpoints each have $99$ other friends among the remaining $197$ people, so their friend sets intersect. Thus three pairwise friends are guaranteed.

For a counterexample to four, split the people into groups of sizes $50,50,99$. Join everyone in the third group to everyone in either of the first two groups; also put a perfect matching between the first two groups. Put no friendships inside any group. Every person has degree100, yet every four people contain two in the same group, who are not friends. Thus exactly $\boxed{k=2,3}$ are guaranteed.''')
norway(2010,'2010-03-11')
ex('1(a)',3.5,'Two right triangles are similar, giving (a+b−p)p=ab and hence (a−p)(p−b)=0.')
ex('1(b)',3.5,'One similar-triangle ratio determines the diagonal intersection height; subtracting two triangle areas gives1/(1+a)−1/2.')
ex('2(a)',2.5,'Cauchy–Schwarz in Engel form immediately gives x²/(1−x)+(1−x)²/x≥(x+1−x)²=1.')
ex('2(b)',3.5,'Use ab+bc+ca≥9abc from the normalized reciprocal-sum inequality and a²+b²+c²≥1/3, then multiply.')
ex('3(a)',3.5,'Pair the sixteen solve-patterns with their complements; pigeonhole among eight paired classes yields the required alternative.')
q('3(b)',4,'Combinatorics',r'A school has $100$ students and $k$ clubs. For every choice of three students, some club contains at least one but not all three. What is the smallest possible $k$?','Membership vectors translate the separation condition into a bound of two students per binary pattern, and the same encoding supplies a sharp construction.',['Pigeonhole principle','Algorithms & constructions','Counting'],r'''Describe each student by a binary vector of length $k$, indicating club memberships. The condition fails exactly when three students have the same vector. Thus every vector can occur at most twice, and $100\le2\cdot2^k$, requiring $k\ge6$.

With six clubs there are64 vectors. Assign the100 students to these vectors with at most two students per vector. Any three then include two with different vectors, so some coordinate—and thus some club—contains one but not the other. It therefore contains at least one but not all three. Hence $\boxed{k=6}$.''')
ex('4(a)',2,'Factor (k−l)(k+l)=1005 and enumerate its four positive factor pairs.')
q('4(b)',5.5,'Geometry',r'For every integer $n>2$, construct $n$ points in the plane that are not all collinear and whose pairwise distances are integers.','A rational Pythagorean parametrization gives many collinear points at rational distances from one off-line point; clearing all denominators finishes the construction.',['Coordinates & vectors','Algorithms & constructions','Diophantine equations'],r'''Take $P=(0,1)$ and, for $t=1,2,\ldots,n-1$, take $Q_t=((t-1/t)/2,0)$. These are distinct points on the horizontal axis, and
$$PQ_t^2=1+\left(\frac{t-1/t}{2}\right)^2=\left(\frac{t+1/t}{2}\right)^2.$$
Thus all $PQ_t$ and all horizontal distances $Q_sQ_t$ are rational. Scale the whole configuration by $2\operatorname{lcm}(1,2,\ldots,n-1)$. Every coordinate and every pairwise distance becomes integral. The points remain distinct, and $P$ remains off the line containing the other $n-1\ge2$ points.''')
